      "page_start": 224,
      "page_end": 224,
      "content": {
        "raw_text": "\n--- Page 224 ---\n125.]\nCONCENTRIC SPHERICAL SURFACES.\n189\nThe solution of this is\n$$V=C_{1}+C_{2} r^{-1}$$\nand the conditions that $V=A$ when $r=a$, and $V=B$ when $r=b$,\ngive for the space between the spherical surfaces,\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n& V=\\frac{A a-B b}{a-b}+\\frac{A-B}{a^{-1}-b^{-1}} r^{-1} \\\\\n& R=-\\frac{d V}{d r}=\\frac{A-B}{a^{-1}-b^{-1}} r^{-2}\n\\end{aligned}$$\nIf $\\sigma_{1}, \\sigma_{2}$ are the surface-densities on the opposed surfaces of a\nsolid sphere of radius $\\alpha$, and a spherical hollow of radius $b$, then\n$$\\sigma_{1}=\\begin{array}{cc}\n1 & A-B \\\\\n4 \\pi a^{2} a^{-1}-b^{-1}\n\\end{array}, \\quad \\sigma_{2}=\\frac{1}{4 \\pi b^{2}} \\frac{B-A}{a^{-1}-b^{-1}} .$$\nIf $e_{1}$ and $e_{2}$ are the whole charges of electricity on these\nsurfaces,\n$$e_{1}=4 \\pi a^{2} \\sigma_{1}=\\frac{A-B}{a^{-1}-b^{-1}}=-e_{2}$$\nThe capacity of the enclosed sphere is therefore $\\frac{a b}{b-a}$.\nIf the outer surface of the shell is also spherical and of radius $c$,\nthen, if there are no other conductors in the neighbourhood, the\ncharge on the outer surface is\n$$e_{3}=B c .$$\nHence the whole charge on the inner sphere is\n$$e_{1}=\\frac{a b}{b-a}(A-B)$$\nand that on the outer shell\n$$e_{2}+e_{3}=\\frac{a b}{b-a}(B-A)+B c .$$\nIf we put $b=\\infty$, we have the case of a sphere in an infinite\nspace. The electric capacity of such a sphere is $c$, or it is\nnumerically equal to its radius.\nThe electric tension on the inner sphere per unit of area is\n$$p=\\frac{1}{8 \\pi} \\frac{b^{2}}{a^{2}} \\frac{(A-B)^{2}}{(b-a)^{2}}$$\nThe resultant of this tension over a hemisphere is $\\pi a^{2} p=F^{\\prime}$\nnormal to the base of the hemisphere, and if this is balanced by\na surface tension exerted across the circular boundary of the\nhemisphere, the tension on unit of length being $T$, we have\n$$F=2 \\pi a T .$$\n",
        "mathpix_markdown": "\n--- Page 224 ---\n\n\nThe solution of this is\n\n$$V=C_{1}+C_{2} r^{-1}$$\n\nand the conditions that $V=A$ when $r=a$, and $V=B$ when $r=b$,\n give for the space between the spherical surfaces,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n& V=\\frac{A a-B b}{a-b}+\\frac{A-B}{a^{-1}-b^{-1}} r^{-1} \\\\\n& R=-\\frac{d V}{d r}=\\frac{A-B}{a^{-1}-b^{-1}} r^{-2}\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\n\nIf $\\sigma_{1}, \\sigma_{2}$ are the surface-densities on the opposed surfaces of a\n solid sphere of radius $\\alpha$, and a spherical hollow of radius $b$, then\n\n$$\\sigma_{1}=\\begin{array}{cc}\n1 & A-B \\\\\n4 \\pi a^{2} a^{-1}-b^{-1}\n\\end{array}, \\quad \\sigma_{2}=\\frac{1}{4 \\pi b^{2}} \\frac{B-A}{a^{-1}-b^{-1}} .$$\n\n\nIf $e_{1}$ and $e_{2}$ are the whole charges of electricity on these\n surfaces,\n\n$$e_{1}=4 \\pi a^{2} \\sigma_{1}=\\frac{A-B}{a^{-1}-b^{-1}}=-e_{2}$$\n\n\nThe capacity of the enclosed sphere is therefore $\\frac{a b}{b-a}$.\n\nIf the outer surface of the shell is also spherical and of radius $c$,\n then, if there are no other conductors in the neighbourhood, the\n charge on the outer surface is\n\n$$e_{3}=B c .$$\n\n\nHence the whole charge on the inner sphere is\n\n$$e_{1}=\\frac{a b}{b-a}(A-B)$$\n\nand that on the outer shell\n\n$$e_{2}+e_{3}=\\frac{a b}{b-a}(B-A)+B c .$$\n\n\nIf we put $b=\\infty$, we have the case of a sphere in an infinite\n space. The electric capacity of such a sphere is $c$, or it is\n numerically equal to its radius.\n\n\nThe electric tension on the inner sphere per unit of area is\n\n$$p=\\frac{1}{8 \\pi} \\frac{b^{2}}{a^{2}} \\frac{(A-B)^{2}}{(b-a)^{2}}$$\n\n\nThe resultant of this tension over a hemisphere is $\\pi a^{2} p=F^{\\prime}$\n normal to the base of the hemisphere, and if this is balanced by\n a surface tension exerted across the circular boundary of the\n hemisphere, the tension on unit of length being $T$, we have\n\n$$F=2 \\pi a T .$$\n",
        "structured_lines": [
          {
